Well, originally "o gigas" (γίγας) is not written with a 'G', rather
with a Gamma ("γάμμα", 'Γ').[*] Few countries in the world seem to
have an appropriate counterpart of that letter with the appropriate
pronunciation. Once that "Gamma" is transcribed to any Latin based
language using a 'G' all sorts of inaccuracies and local conventions
in the pronunciation pop in.

It's interesting, though, that the /jija/ (NOT /jiga/) pronunciation
is very close to how it is (nowadays) spoken in Greece; but the 'j'
is not pronounced like the English 'j' as in "jet", rather like the
German 'j' as in "jubeln"; so (the German spoken) "jija" is closer
to the Greek one than the English spoken "jija", and also closer to
the German spoken "Giga" (with a hard 'G') or, _if_ that's the case,
the English spoken "Jiga".

Confused? - Apply a unihorn!

Janis

[*] Note that in either language, Greek and Latin based ones, you
have two 'Γ', resp. two 'G' in that word. Both characters in each
respective word are, in German resp. in Greek, pronounced the same.
That's certainly much clearer and causes less confusion than with
conventions to cover language inconsistencies in other languages.


